<div id="legend">
<div class="leg" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;"><strong style="color:#00180f;">RUN THIS WEEK</strong><br/>5–8 minutes. Open cold and follow the numbered steps.</div>
<div class="leg" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">LOW PREP / LINK</strong><br/>Still under 10 minutes; open the clip, Reflect activity or simple resource named on the card.</div>
<div class="leg" style="border-left:2px solid #7a9b96;padding-left:10px;"><strong style="color:#557b76;font-size:11px;">ACCESSIBILITY</strong><br/><span style="font-size:11px;color:#666;">Built-in alternatives. “Pass” is always acceptable.</span></div>
</div><div id="teacher-tip" style="margin:-6px 0 14px;background:#fff;border:1px solid #ddd;border-radius:4px;padding:8px 11px;font-size:12px;line-height:1.4;color:#444;"><strong>For teachers:</strong> Run the card marked <strong>THIS WEEK</strong>. The activity is designed to work without a worksheet, student device or advance setup unless the card explicitly says otherwise. Greet each student by name as they arrive, before the activity starts — five seconds each, and it does more for belonging than any single activity below. When a card says ‘Pairs’ or ‘Groups of N’, use it now and then to cross year levels instead of defaulting to friendship groups — this Home Group’s mixed 7–12 membership is a genuine asset most schools don’t have.</div>
<div class="panel" id="panel-0" style="display:none;"><div style="background:#00180f;padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:12px;border-radius:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;color:#7aaa7a;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Term 1  ·  Weeks 1–2</p><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:22px;font-weight:700;color:#f2b400;">Welcome back &amp; Positive Education intro</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#a8c5a0;">Weekly focuses: Welcome back · Positive Education intro</p></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="1" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;">
<p class="lesson-label" style="margin:0 0 5px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#00180f;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 1 — RUN THIS — 6 MIN — NO PREP</p>
<p class="activity-title" style="margin:0 0 7px;font-size:15px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.35;color:#1a1a1a;">Pick a Side: Welcome Back</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 9px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#333;">Fast choices get everyone moving or voting before the conversation becomes personal.</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Call 4–5 choices: early bird / night owl; beach / bush; music / sport; work alone / work with others. Students move left/right or vote with hands.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Finish with: ‘For Home Group, what matters more — calm, fun, belonging or getting organised?’ Take a few reasons.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">3.</span>Agree on one tiny Home Group habit for the year: how you start, include people or reset after a rough morning.</p>
<p class="takeaway" style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Connection comes before content. A Home Group works better when students have quick, low-risk ways to learn about each other.</p>
<p class="fallback" style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> No room to move? Keep everyone seated and vote left/right with two hands.</p>
</div><details class="more-options"><summary>Optional swap for this week</summary><div class="lesson-card" data-optional="true" data-week="1" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;">
<p class="lesson-label" style="margin:0 0 5px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 1 — OPTIONAL SWAP — 6 MIN — NO PAPER</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 7px;font-size:15px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.35;color:#1a1a1a;">Three Connections Challenge</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 9px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#333;">Students have four minutes to find three different people who match three prompts — no grid and no signatures.</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Call three prompts, e.g. ‘likes the same music as you’, ‘has tried a sport you haven’t’, ‘can name all six houses’.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Students circulate and find one different person for each prompt.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">3.</span>Ask: what was the most unexpected connection?</p>
<p class="takeaway" style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Small shared interests make a large group feel less anonymous.</p>
<p class="fallback" style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Keep students seated and run it with the three nearest people.</p>
</div></details><div class="lesson-card" data-week="2" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;">
<p class="lesson-label" style="margin:0 0 5px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#00180f;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 2 — RUN THIS — 6 MIN — NO PREP</p>
<p class="activity-title" style="margin:0 0 7px;font-size:15px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.35;color:#1a1a1a;">PERMAH Speed Round</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 9px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#333;">Turn PERMAH into a quick classification game rather than a mini-lecture.</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Put P-E-R-M-A-H on the board and number them 1–6.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Call everyday moments: laughing with a friend, getting absorbed in a game, helping someone, working toward a goal, sleeping well. Students show 1–6 fingers for the best fit.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">3.</span>Finish: each person picks one pillar they could improve this week and one tiny action.</p>
<p class="takeaway" style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Wellbeing is something people can practise through small choices, not just something they either have or do not have.</p>
<p class="fallback" style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Skip the board: say the six words once and let students call the pillar aloud.</p>
</div><details class="more-options"><summary>Optional swap for this week</summary><div class="lesson-card" data-optional="true" data-week="2" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 2 — OPTIONAL SWAP — 6 MIN — NO PREP</p><p style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:15px;font-weight:700;color:#1a1a1a;">Two Truths and a Wish</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:18px;font-size:13px;color:#333;line-height:1.5;"><li style="margin-bottom:4px;">In pairs, each person shares two true things about themselves and one genuine wish for the year — in any order, not labelled.</li><li style="margin-bottom:4px;">Partner guesses which of the three is the wish. No wrong guesses — the point is listening, not winning.</li><li style="margin-bottom:4px;">Link back to PERMAH on the board: a wish is a form of Meaning or Accomplishment — noticing what you’re aiming for is the first step toward it.</li></ul></div></details><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;">All activities can be run verbally. Students can respond silently, with a partner, by thumbs/fingers, or by opting out of personal sharing.</p></div></div><div class="panel" id="panel-1" style="display:none;"><div style="background:#00180f;padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:12px;border-radius:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;color:#7aaa7a;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Term 1  ·  Weeks 3–4</p><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:22px;font-weight:700;color:#f2b400;">PERMAH overview &amp; Virtues &amp; character strengths</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#a8c5a0;">Weekly focuses: PERMAH overview · Virtues &amp; character strengths</p></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="1" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;">
<p class="lesson-label" style="margin:0 0 5px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#00180f;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 3 — RUN THIS — 5 MIN — NO PREP</p>
<p class="activity-title" style="margin:0 0 7px;font-size:15px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.35;color:#1a1a1a;">PERMAH: Beat the Teacher</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 9px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#333;">The class tries to classify six school moments correctly before the teacher can catch an uncertain answer.</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Number PERMAH 1–6 on the board.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Call six quick examples. Everyone shows the number at once; ask one student to justify the majority answer.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">3.</span>If the explanation makes sense, the class gets the point. Aim for 6/6.</p>
<p class="takeaway" style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>The same activity can support more than one PERMAH pillar; the useful part is explaining the connection.</p>
<p class="fallback" style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> No scoring needed — just rapid-fire six examples.</p>
</div><details class="more-options"><summary>Optional swap for this week</summary><div class="lesson-card" data-optional="true" data-week="1" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 3 — OPTIONAL SWAP — 7 MIN — BOARD</p><p style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:15px;font-weight:700;color:#1a1a1a;">PERMAH Pictionary</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:18px;font-size:13px;color:#333;line-height:1.5;"><li style="margin-bottom:4px;">Write the six PERMAH letters on the board. In teams, one student draws a simple scene showing one pillar in action (no words, no letters) while their team guesses which pillar it is.</li><li style="margin-bottom:4px;">Rotate the drawer each round; keep a running score by team.</li><li style="margin-bottom:4px;">After each round, ask the team: “What’s one real example of that pillar from this week?”</li></ul></div></details><div class="lesson-card" data-week="2" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;">
<p class="lesson-label" style="margin:0 0 5px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#00180f;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 4 — RUN THIS — 6 MIN — NO PREP</p>
<p class="activity-title" style="margin:0 0 7px;font-size:15px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.35;color:#1a1a1a;">Strength Detective</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 9px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#333;">Students identify strengths from short school scenarios, then invent one scenario of their own.</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Read examples: ‘keeps trying after two wrong answers’; ‘invites someone into the group’; ‘admits they made the mistake’; ‘asks a question nobody else thought of’.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Pairs name the strength as fast as they can.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">3.</span>Each pair invents one scenario; the room guesses the strength.</p>
<p class="takeaway" style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Character strengths become useful when students can recognise what they look like in ordinary behaviour.</p>
<p class="fallback" style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> If names are forgotten, accept plain-English answers such as persistence, honesty or kindness.</p>
</div><details class="more-options"><summary>Optional swap for this week</summary><div class="lesson-card" data-optional="true" data-week="2" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 4 — OPTIONAL SWAP — 7 MIN — STICKY NOTES</p><p style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:15px;font-weight:700;color:#1a1a1a;">Celebrity Heads: Strengths edition</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:18px;font-size:13px;color:#333;line-height:1.5;"><li style="margin-bottom:4px;">Before class, write one character strength already introduced (Honesty, Kindness, Curiosity, Teamwork…) on each of a few sticky notes. Hold one above a volunteer’s head, unseen by them.</li><li style="margin-bottom:4px;">The volunteer asks the group yes/no questions to work out which strength they are — “Do people notice when I’m missing?” “Am I about how you treat other people?”</li><li style="margin-bottom:4px;">Swap volunteers every 90 seconds; play as many rounds as time allows.</li></ul></div></details><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;">All activities can be run verbally. Students can respond silently, with a partner, by thumbs/fingers, or by opting out of personal sharing.</p></div></div><div class="panel" id="panel-2" style="display:none;"><div style="background:#00180f;padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:12px;border-radius:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;color:#7aaa7a;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Term 1  ·  Weeks 5–6</p><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:22px;font-weight:700;color:#f2b400;">Humanity &amp; Love</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#a8c5a0;">Virtue: Humanity · Strength: Love</p></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="1" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#00180f;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 5 — RUN THIS — 6 MIN — NO PREP</p><p class="activity-title">Common Ground</p><p class="lesson-lead" style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:13px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.4;color:#1a1a1a;">Groups of 4. Find three things every single person in the group has in common — not obvious ones (school, uniform). Genuine connections only.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Give the group 90 seconds to find 3 shared things — not clothing, not school, not being in this room. Compare with another group: did anyone find something surprising?</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>The easy connections are the visible ones. Humanity is the virtue; Love is one of the strengths that helps people build deeper connection.</p><p style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#996600;">► </strong>Every stranger has more in common with you than you’d guess from the outside. That’s Humanity in action — assuming connection is there, and looking for it.</p><p style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Group stuck? Suggest lighter categories: a food you all like, a place you have all been, a subject you all enjoy, a morning habit, or something that reliably makes you laugh.</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students who prefer not to speak in a group of 4 can write their three shared items and pass the note around instead of saying them aloud.</li></ul></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="2" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 6 — RUN THIS — 7 MIN — SHORT CLIP</p><p class="activity-title">Empathy: Fix It or Feel It?</p><p class="lesson-lead" style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:13px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.4;color:#1a1a1a;">Play: Brené Brown on Empathy — <a href="https://youtu.be/1Evwgu369Jw" style="color:#1565c0;text-decoration:underline;" target="_blank">youtu.be/1Evwgu369Jw</a> (2:53). Pairs discuss: what's the difference between empathy and sympathy?</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Play the clip (2:53). Then give a fictional scenario: “A student has just been dropped from a team they trained hard for.” Pairs invent one sympathetic response and one empathetic response.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Share two or three responses. Ask: which response would make the person feel understood rather than fixed?</p><p style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Empathy is noticing another person’s experience and responding in a way that shows you are trying to understand. Love and Kindness make that visible.</p><p style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Video won't load? Use a fictional example: a student has been dropped from a team they trained hard for. What would sympathy sound like? What would empathy sound like?</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students can describe a character from a book or show rather than sharing personally.</li></ul></div></div><div class="panel" id="panel-3" style="display:none;"><div style="background:#00180f;padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:12px;border-radius:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;color:#7aaa7a;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Term 1  ·  Weeks 7–8</p><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:22px;font-weight:700;color:#f2b400;">Social Intelligence &amp; Kindness</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#a8c5a0;">Virtue: Humanity · Strengths: Social Intelligence · Kindness</p></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="1" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#00180f;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 7 — RUN THIS — 6 MIN — NO PREP</p><p class="activity-title">Feeling Charades</p><p class="lesson-lead" style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:13px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.4;color:#1a1a1a;">One student at a time acts out an emotion silently — no words, no sound effects — while the group calls out guesses.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Teacher (or a volunteer) whispers an emotion to the actor: nervous, proud, homesick, relieved, embarrassed, hopeful. 30 seconds to act it out, no talking.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>First correct guesser describes what gave it away — was it the face, the posture, the energy?</p><p style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#996600;">► </strong>Reading feelings from the outside is exactly what Social Intelligence and Kindness run on — you can’t help someone until you notice what they’re actually feeling.</p><p style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Running out of ideas? Use exam week, first day back, and grand final loss as ready-made scenarios.</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students who don’t want to perform can whisper their guess to a partner instead of calling out, or take a turn describing rather than acting.</li></ul></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="2" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;">
<p class="lesson-label" style="margin:0 0 5px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 8 — RUN THIS — 6 MIN — NO PREP</p>
<p class="activity-title" style="margin:0 0 7px;font-size:15px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.35;color:#1a1a1a;">Emotion Detective: Don’t Jump to Conclusions</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 9px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#333;">One behaviour can have several explanations. Students practise staying curious instead of deciding they “know” how someone feels.</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Read: ‘Someone gets their test back, looks at it, turns it face down and says nothing.’</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Pairs have 30 seconds to produce three possible feelings — not one.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">3.</span>Ask: what could you say or ask that would help without assuming? Repeat with: ‘A friend walks past you without saying hello.’</p>
<p class="takeaway" style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Social Intelligence is partly the ability to read cues while remembering that your first interpretation might be wrong.</p>
<p class="fallback" style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Give four choices — embarrassed, angry, disappointed, relieved — and vote by fingers.</p>
</div><div class="access-note" style="border-left:2px solid #7a9b96;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;">
<p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p>
<ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.45;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students can contribute a feeling word, a question or simply vote by fingers.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">EALD students can use a home-language word first and translate with a partner if useful.</li></ul></div></div><div class="panel" id="panel-4" style="display:none;"><div style="background:#00180f;padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:12px;border-radius:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;color:#7aaa7a;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Term 1  ·  Weeks 9–10</p><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:22px;font-weight:700;color:#f2b400;">Transcendence &amp; Humour</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#a8c5a0;">Virtue: Transcendence · Strength: Humour</p></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="1" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#00180f;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 9 — RUN THIS — 5 MIN — NO PREP</p><p class="activity-title">Awe Hunt</p><p class="lesson-lead">A 60-second challenge to notice something most people in the room have stopped seeing.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Give everyone 20 seconds to scan the room, view outside or a familiar object and choose one detail they think most people have overlooked.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Pairs compare. If both picked the same thing, each gets 15 seconds to find a more unusual detail.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">3.</span>Take 4–5 nominations. The room votes for the most surprising thing that was there the whole time.</p><p class="takeaway" style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Awe can begin with attention. The Transcendence virtue includes strengths such as Appreciation of Beauty and Excellence — noticing more can change how ordinary surroundings feel.</p><p class="fallback" style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> No good visual details? Switch to sound: 20 seconds of silence, then name a sound you normally tune out.</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students who don’t want to share aloud can write their word instead and hold it up, or simply say ‘pass’.</li></ul></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="2" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 10 — RUN THIS — 7 MIN — SHORT CLIP</p><p class="activity-title">Humour with a Point</p><p class="lesson-lead" style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:13px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.4;color:#1a1a1a;">Play: Kid President — How to Change the World — <a href="https://youtu.be/4z7gDsSKUmU" style="color:#1565c0;text-decoration:underline;" target="_blank">youtu.be/4z7gDsSKUmU</a> (3:30). What's the serious point behind the humour?</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Play the clip (3:30). Pairs: 'Where did you laugh — and what was the serious point behind that moment?' 60 seconds.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>The Humour strength does something argument can't — it lowers the guard. What would be harder to say without humour?</p><p style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#996600;">► </strong>Humour isn't trivial — it changes the temperature of a room and gets serious things said. That's a real strength.</p><p style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Video won't load? Ask: 'When did you last use humour to make a difficult moment easier? What made it work rather than backfire?'</p></div><div class="access-note" style="border-left:2px solid #7a9b96;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;">
<p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p>
<ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.45;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students can discuss the clip without sharing a personal example.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">If humour is likely to become performative, keep the prompt focused on what the humour allowed the speaker to communicate.</li></ul></div></div><div class="panel" id="panel-5" style="display:none;"><div style="background:#00180f;padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:12px;border-radius:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;color:#7aaa7a;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Term 2  ·  Weeks 1–2</p><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:22px;font-weight:700;color:#f2b400;">Spirituality / Meaning &amp; Appreciation of Beauty</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#a8c5a0;">Virtue: Transcendence · Strengths: Spirituality / Meaning · Appreciation of Beauty</p></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="1" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#00180f;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 1 — RUN THIS — 5 MIN — NO PREP</p><p class="activity-title">Hidden Design Challenge</p><p class="lesson-lead">Find one ordinary thing in the room that is better designed than people usually notice.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Pairs get 30 seconds to choose one object or feature — a chair, hinge, bag, shoe, window, sign, pen or piece of clothing.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>They get another 30 seconds to identify one design choice that makes it work well: shape, texture, placement, material, colour or simplicity.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">3.</span>Hear 4–5 examples. No repeats. Vote for the detail the class is least likely to have noticed before.</p><p class="takeaway" style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Appreciation of Beauty and Excellence is not only about scenery or art. It includes noticing skill, design and quality in ordinary things.</p><p class="fallback" style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Use the nearest object: everyone looks at a pen, chair or shoe and names one design feature that has a purpose.</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students who prefer not to speak off the cuff can write their word rather than saying it aloud.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">EALD students can respond in point form.</li></ul></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="2" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 2 — RUN THIS — 7 MIN — OPEN REFLECT</p><p class="activity-title">Best Possible Selves</p><p class="lesson-lead" style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:13px;font-weight:400;color:#1a1a1a;">Microsoft Reflect: Best Possible Selves<span style="font-size:12px;font-weight:400;color:#666;margin-left:8px;">reflect.microsoft.com/app/brainbreak/bestPossibleSelves</span></p><p style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:15px;line-height:1.5;color:#1a1a1a;">Open on the classroom screen. A guided reflection on the best version of yourself — directly aligned with Purpose and Transcendence.</p><div style="clear:both;"></div><div style="border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);margin-top:10px;padding-top:8px;"><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;color:#666;">Why this lesson:</strong> Signature Transcendence activity — visualising purpose and best possible self.</p><a href="https://reflect.microsoft.com/app/brainbreak/bestPossibleSelves" style="display:inline-block;margin-top:10px;background:#1565c0;color:#fff;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;padding:5px 13px;border-radius:4px;text-decoration:none;" target="_blank">▶ Open Reflect Activity</a></div><p style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Reflect unavailable? Pairs answer: “If next year went well, what would be one small sign that you were becoming more like the person you want to be?”</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">For students who find future-focused thinking anxiety-provoking: 'This isn't about pressure to be perfect — just notice what matters to you right now.'</li></ul></div></div><div class="panel" id="panel-6" style="display:none;"><div style="background:#00180f;padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:12px;border-radius:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;color:#7aaa7a;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Term 2  ·  Weeks 3–4</p><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:22px;font-weight:700;color:#f2b400;">Hope &amp; Gratitude</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#a8c5a0;">Virtue: Transcendence · Strengths: Hope · Gratitude</p></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="1" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#00180f;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 3 — RUN THIS — 6 MIN — NO PREP</p><p class="activity-title">Unsung Hero</p><p class="lesson-lead">Who makes school work better without getting much attention for it?</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Pairs have 45 seconds to nominate one person or role in the school that is easy to overlook — cleaner, grounds staff, office staff, teacher aide, librarian, coach, student helper or someone else.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>The nomination only counts if the pair can name one specific thing that person does that makes somebody else’s day easier.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">3.</span>Take nominations and build a quick top three. Finish by asking: what would change if nobody did those jobs for a week?</p><p class="takeaway" style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Gratitude becomes more powerful when it is specific. Noticing contribution is the first step; expressing it is the next.</p><p class="fallback" style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Teacher models one unsung contribution, then asks the room for two more.</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students who won't name someone aloud can write it on a slip of paper — teacher reads them anonymously.</li></ul></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="2" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;">
<p class="lesson-label" style="margin:0 0 5px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 4 — RUN THIS — 6 MIN — NO PREP</p>
<p class="activity-title" style="margin:0 0 7px;font-size:15px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.35;color:#1a1a1a;">Back / Forward</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 9px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#333;">A fast paired reflection: one thing worth keeping from this term and one thing worth moving toward next term.</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Pairs. Person A gets 45 seconds: one thing they are glad happened this term and why it mattered.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Same person gets 45 seconds: one thing they hope is different next term and the first small step toward it. Swap.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">3.</span>Take 3–4 volunteers: share only the small first step, not the personal story.</p>
<p class="takeaway" style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Gratitude looks back at what mattered; Hope looks forward and identifies a path. Both become more useful when they are specific.</p>
<p class="fallback" style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Whole room: one-word win, one-word hope. Students may pass.</p>
</div><div class="access-note" style="border-left:2px solid #7a9b96;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;">
<p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p>
<ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.45;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students can speak about the class, a subject or a hobby rather than something personal.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">No writing or public sharing is required.</li></ul></div></div><div class="panel" id="panel-7" style="display:none;"><div style="background:#00180f;padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:12px;border-radius:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;color:#7aaa7a;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Term 2  ·  Weeks 5–6</p><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:22px;font-weight:700;color:#f2b400;">Wisdom &amp; Love of Learning</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#a8c5a0;">Virtue: Wisdom · Strength: Love of Learning</p></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="1" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#00180f;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 5 — RUN THIS — 6 MIN — NO PREP</p><p class="activity-title">Question Battle</p><p class="lesson-lead">The goal is not to know the answer — it is to produce the question that makes the room most curious.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Pairs get 45 seconds to invent one question they would genuinely like answered. It can be about science, people, sport, technology, school, history or anything else.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Pair up pairs. Each reads its question; the group of four chooses the one that would be hardest to ignore.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">3.</span>Hear the finalists. Vote for the question the class would most want ten minutes to investigate.</p><p class="takeaway" style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Love of Learning often starts before an answer exists. A strong question gives curiosity somewhere to go.</p><p class="fallback" style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Teacher offers two questions and the class votes which one they would rather investigate — then asks for one challenger.</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">EALD students can write their question in any language and point to key words.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Reassure students: ‘It can be anything — science, people, how something works. Doesn’t have to be deep.’</li></ul></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="2" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;">
<p class="lesson-label" style="margin:0 0 5px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 6 — RUN THIS — 6 MIN — SCREEN ONLY</p>
<p class="activity-title" style="margin:0 0 7px;font-size:15px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.35;color:#1a1a1a;">How Many Squares?</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 9px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#333;">Put the grid on screen and ask for the total number of squares. The obvious answer is not the complete answer.</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Give 30 seconds silently. Everyone commits to a number with fingers or calls it to a partner.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Pairs get 90 seconds to prove their answer. Ask a few groups for totals and reasoning.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">3.</span>Reveal: 14 — nine 1×1 squares, four 2×2 squares and one 3×3 square.</p>
<div aria-label="A three by three grid of equal squares" class="square-puzzle" role="img"><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
<p class="takeaway" style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Love of Learning often starts when the first answer stops being enough. Good thinking keeps checking the structure rather than settling for the obvious count.</p>
<p class="fallback" style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Draw a simple 3×3 noughts-and-crosses grid on the board. Same puzzle.</p>
</div><div class="access-note" style="border-left:2px solid #7a9b96;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;">
<p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p>
<ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.45;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students can count aloud with a partner instead of recording anything.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">For students who need more structure, prompt: “Count the smallest squares first, then look for bigger ones.”</li></ul></div></div><div class="panel" id="panel-8" style="display:none;"><div style="background:#00180f;padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:12px;border-radius:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;color:#7aaa7a;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Term 2  ·  Weeks 7–8</p><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:22px;font-weight:700;color:#f2b400;">Perspective &amp; Curiosity</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#a8c5a0;">Virtue: Wisdom · Strengths: Perspective · Curiosity</p></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="1" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;">
<p class="lesson-label" style="margin:0 0 5px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#00180f;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 7 — RUN THIS — 6 MIN — NO PREP</p>
<p class="activity-title" style="margin:0 0 7px;font-size:15px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.35;color:#1a1a1a;">Three Possible Stories</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 9px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#333;">A tiny ambiguity challenge that rewards students for generating more than one explanation.</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Read: ‘You message a friend after school. They read it and do not reply for six hours.’ Pairs have 30 seconds to generate three plausible explanations.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Take answers. Then ask: which explanation did your brain jump to first? What evidence did you actually have?</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">3.</span>Repeat with: ‘A teacher walks past you in the corridor without responding when you say hello.’</p>
<p class="takeaway" style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Perspective asks “what else could be true?” Curiosity keeps the question open long enough to avoid turning one clue into a whole story.</p>
<p class="fallback" style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Use any ambiguous everyday event and require three explanations before anyone can choose one.</p>
</div><div class="access-note" style="border-left:2px solid #7a9b96;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;">
<p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p>
<ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.45;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students do not need to discuss an actual friendship or conflict; the scenarios are fictional.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students can contribute one explanation rather than three.</li></ul></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="2" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 8 — RUN THIS — 7 MIN — CUE CLIP</p><p class="activity-title">Single Story: What Are You Missing?</p><p class="lesson-lead" style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:13px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.4;color:#1a1a1a;">Play: Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie — The Danger of a Single Story — <a href="https://youtu.be/D9Ihs241zeg" style="color:#1565c0;text-decoration:underline;" target="_blank">youtu.be/D9Ihs241zeg</a> — play the roommate section only (about 2 minutes). One thing that stayed with you.</p><p style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:12px;line-height:1.5;color:#7a5800;background:#fff5d6;border-radius:3px;padding:7px 10px;"><strong>Cue it before class.</strong> The full talk runs 18:33 — far too long for Home Group, so do not start at 0:00. Scrub to roughly 4:20, where she says <em>‘I was 19’</em> and starts on her American university roommate, and stop about two minutes later on <em>‘my roommate had a single story of Africa’</em>. Those two lines are the excerpt — nudge the start point until the first one is where you begin.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Play the roommate excerpt (about 2 minutes). Rapid-fire around the room — one thing each. No repeating what someone already said.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>What is a harmless example of a person, place or group being reduced to one story? The Perspective strength asks what information is missing.</p><p style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#996600;">► </strong>Perspective means asking: what am I missing? Where do I only have one version of something? That's not a comfortable question — but it's the right one.</p><p style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Video won't load, or the excerpt won't cue in time? 'A single story is when you only know one thing about a person or place — and that one thing becomes the whole story. Where do you have one?'</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Pause once part-way through the excerpt and check comprehension: 'What's the single story her roommate had about her?</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Can anyone say it in one sentence?' before continuing.</li></ul></div></div><div class="panel" id="panel-9" style="display:none;"><div style="background:#00180f;padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:12px;border-radius:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;color:#7aaa7a;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Term 2  ·  Weeks 9–10</p><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:22px;font-weight:700;color:#f2b400;">Creativity &amp; Judgement</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#a8c5a0;">Virtue: Wisdom · Strengths: Creativity · Judgement</p></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="1" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#00180f;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 9 — RUN THIS — 6 MIN — NO PREP</p><p class="activity-title">Strengths Pictionary</p><p class="lesson-lead" style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:13px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.4;color:#1a1a1a;">One student draws a character strength on the board (no letters, no numbers) while the group calls out guesses.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Teacher whispers or shows a strength card to the drawer: Honesty, Teamwork, Curiosity, Humour, Fairness. 45 seconds to draw it — no talking, no writing words.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>First correct guesser explains what image made them think of it — there’s rarely only one right way to draw a strength.</p><p style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#996600;">► </strong>Turning an idea into an image no one’s seen before is Creativity. Judging which parts of the drawing actually communicate the idea — and which don’t — is Judgement. You need both to make yourself understood.</p><p style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Drawing stalls? Allow one gesture-only clue after 20 seconds with no guesses.</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students who don’t want to draw can act as the guesser every round, or describe what to draw to a scribe instead of drawing themselves.</li></ul></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="2" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;">
<p class="lesson-label" style="margin:0 0 5px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 10 — RUN THIS — 7 MIN — NO PREP</p>
<p class="activity-title" style="margin:0 0 7px;font-size:15px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.35;color:#1a1a1a;">Worst Idea Wins</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 9px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#333;">Starting with deliberately terrible ideas makes creativity easier and gives Judgement something concrete to improve.</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Challenge: ‘How could we make Home Group absolutely terrible?’ Groups get 60 seconds to invent the worst realistic idea they can.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Hear 3–4. Then each group must flip one bad idea into a genuinely useful improvement.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">3.</span>Vote twice: most creative flip and most workable flip.</p>
<p class="takeaway" style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Creativity produces possibilities. Judgement tests which possibilities are worth using. Strong ideas need both.</p>
<p class="fallback" style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Use a smaller prompt: “How could we make the first five minutes of school worse?” Then flip it.</p>
</div><div class="access-note" style="border-left:2px solid #7a9b96;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;">
<p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p>
<ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.45;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students can nominate ideas without presenting them personally; a spokesperson can share for the group.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Keep “worst ideas” about routines and systems, not individual students or staff.</li></ul></div></div><div class="panel" id="panel-10" style="display:none;"><div style="background:#00180f;padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:12px;border-radius:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;color:#7aaa7a;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Term 3  ·  Weeks 1–2</p><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:22px;font-weight:700;color:#f2b400;">Virtues recap &amp; PERMAH recap</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#a8c5a0;">Weekly focuses: Virtues recap · PERMAH recap</p></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="1" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;">
<p class="lesson-label" style="margin:0 0 5px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#00180f;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 1 — RUN THIS — 6 MIN — NO PREP</p>
<p class="activity-title" style="margin:0 0 7px;font-size:15px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.35;color:#1a1a1a;">PERMAH Lightning Round</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 9px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#333;">A retrieval game: the room tries to identify all six PERMAH pillars from everyday school moments.</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Number P-E-R-M-A-H from 1–6 on the board.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Call six moments: laughing with a friend; losing track of time in an activity; helping at an event; doing something that matters; finishing a hard task; getting enough sleep. Everyone shows the matching number.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">3.</span>Final round: students invent one example and try to make the teacher guess the pillar.</p>
<p class="takeaway" style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>PERMAH is useful when students can recognise the pillars in ordinary life, not just recite the words.</p>
<p class="fallback" style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Skip the board and let students call the pillar aloud.</p>
</div><div class="access-note" style="border-left:2px solid #7a9b96;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;">
<p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p>
<ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.45;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students may respond with fingers, pointing or a partner answer.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Accept more than one pillar when the student can justify the connection.</li></ul></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="2" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;">
<p class="lesson-label" style="margin:0 0 5px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 2 — RUN THIS — 7 MIN — NO PREP</p>
<p class="activity-title" style="margin:0 0 7px;font-size:15px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.35;color:#1a1a1a;">Strengths Face-Off</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 9px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#333;">Pairs choose which strength would help most in a short scenario. There is no single correct answer — the reasoning is the game.</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Read a scenario: ‘You are new to a group where everyone already knows each other.’ Pairs choose one strength that would help most.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Take two competing answers and ask the room to vote for the stronger case.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">3.</span>Repeat with: ‘You failed something you expected to pass’; ‘two friends are arguing’; ‘you have a boring job that still needs finishing’.</p>
<p class="takeaway" style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Character strengths are tools. Different strengths can help with the same problem, and the useful question is why a particular strength fits the moment.</p>
<p class="fallback" style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> If students cannot remember names, allow plain-English strengths and supply the VIA term afterwards.</p>
</div><div class="access-note" style="border-left:2px solid #7a9b96;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;">
<p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p>
<ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.45;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students can vote rather than justify aloud.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Use fictional scenarios only; nobody needs to disclose a current personal problem.</li></ul></div></div><div class="panel" id="panel-11" style="display:none;"><div style="background:#00180f;padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:12px;border-radius:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;color:#7aaa7a;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Term 3  ·  Weeks 3–4</p><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:22px;font-weight:700;color:#f2b400;">Justice &amp; Leadership</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#a8c5a0;">Virtue: Justice · Strength: Leadership</p></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="1" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;">
<p class="lesson-label" style="margin:0 0 5px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#00180f;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 3 — RUN THIS — 6 MIN — NO PREP</p>
<p class="activity-title" style="margin:0 0 7px;font-size:15px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.35;color:#1a1a1a;">Count to 20 — No Leader Allowed</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 9px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#333;">The whole group must count from 1 to 20, but there is no speaking order and nobody may nominate who goes next.</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Everyone faces inward. One person says ‘1’, somebody else says ‘2’, and so on. No gestures or planned order.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>If two people speak at once, restart at 1. Give the group three attempts.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">3.</span>Debrief: what helped? Who led without taking over? What behaviours made space for other people?</p>
<p class="takeaway" style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Leadership is not always the loudest person directing everyone. It can be reading the group, creating space and helping the group succeed.</p>
<p class="fallback" style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Aim for 10 instead of 20 with a large or restless group.</p>
</div><div class="access-note" style="border-left:2px solid #7a9b96;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;">
<p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p>
<ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.45;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students who prefer not to speak can act as the attempt counter or observer and name one helpful group behaviour afterwards.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Avoid singling out students who spoke at the same time; restart neutrally.</li></ul></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="2" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 4 — RUN THIS — 7 MIN — SHORT CLIP</p><p class="activity-title">Champion Effect</p><p class="lesson-lead" style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:13px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.4;color:#1a1a1a;">Play: Rita Pierson — Every Kid Needs a Champion — <a href="https://youtu.be/SFnMTHhKdkw?t=210" style="color:#1565c0;text-decoration:underline;" target="_blank">youtu.be/SFnMTHhKdkw</a> (play 3:30–7:49). Name one person who championed you and what they did. Start at 3:30 — the first half is her background; the part students remember (the “I am somebody” saying and the “+2 and a smiley face” story) is in the second half.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">1.</span> Play 3:30 to the end (7:49). Pairs identify one behaviour from the clip that makes someone feel believed in. Personal examples are optional.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">2.</span> Challenge pairs to turn that behaviour into something a student could actually do this week for someone else.</p><p style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#996600;">► </strong>You don't need a title to lead. You don't need permission to champion someone. The Leadership and Teamwork strengths are about choosing to act — for people who aren't obviously your responsibility.</p><p style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Video won't load? Ask: 'Think of an adult who made you feel like you could do something you didn't think you could. What specifically did they do?'</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Some students may not have experienced being championed.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Offer: 'Or describe what it would look like if someone had — what would they do?'</li></ul></div></div><div class="panel" id="panel-12" style="display:none;"><div style="background:#00180f;padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:12px;border-radius:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;color:#7aaa7a;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Term 3  ·  Weeks 5–6</p><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:22px;font-weight:700;color:#f2b400;">Fairness &amp; Teamwork</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#a8c5a0;">Virtue: Justice · Strengths: Fairness · Teamwork</p></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="1" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#00180f;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 5 — RUN THIS — 6 MIN — NO PREP</p><p class="activity-title">Silent Sort</p><p class="lesson-lead" style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:13px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.4;color:#1a1a1a;">Whole class (or table groups), no talking: sort yourselves into a single line by birthday month, then by house — using only gestures.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Set the rule (e.g. line up in birthday-month order, January to December) and start the clock. No talking, no writing — gestures and mouthing numbers only.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Once settled, check the line out loud. Reset and repeat with a harder rule — house, then alphabetical order by first name — and see if the group is faster the second time.</p><p style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#996600;">► </strong>Nobody can finish this alone — it only works if everyone commits to reading the group and adjusting their own spot. That’s Teamwork. Making sure quieter students aren’t just pushed to whichever end is easiest is Fairness.</p><p style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Class too big to move safely? Sort into table groups of 6–8 instead of the whole room.</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students with mobility restrictions can direct their own placement verbally while others move for them, or the activity can run seated with students holding up written numbers instead of physically lining up.</li></ul></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="2" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 6 — RUN THIS — 7 MIN — SHORT CLIP</p><p class="activity-title">Fairness Monkey</p><p class="lesson-lead" style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:13px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.4;color:#1a1a1a;">Play: Capuchin Monkeys — Unequal Pay — <a href="https://youtu.be/meiU6TxysCg" style="color:#1565c0;text-decoration:underline;" target="_blank">youtu.be/meiU6TxysCg</a> (2:30). What does this tell us about where fairness comes from?</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Play the clip (2:30). Pairs: 'Is the angry monkey right to be angry — or is it just reacting to difference? What's the actual injustice?' 60 seconds.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Humans — and some other primates — can react strongly to unequal treatment. The Fairness strength is what we do with that reaction, especially when the unfairness is not directed at us.</p><p style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#996600;">► </strong>Unequal treatment can trigger a strong reaction. The Fairness strength is noticing, reasoning about and responding to what is fair — especially when someone else is affected.</p><p style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Video won't load? Describe the experiment: two monkeys, same task, one gets a grape, one a cucumber. The cucumber monkey throws it back. Same debrief.</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Prime students before playing: 'You're going to watch two monkeys doing the same job for different pay.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Watch the one on the right.' The clip moves quickly and context helps.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Debrief can use a fictional frame — students don't need to name personal experiences of unfairness.</li></ul></div></div><div class="panel" id="panel-13" style="display:none;"><div style="background:#00180f;padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:12px;border-radius:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;color:#7aaa7a;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Term 3  ·  Weeks 7–8</p><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:22px;font-weight:700;color:#f2b400;">Courage &amp; Zest</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#a8c5a0;">Virtue: Courage · Strength: Zest</p></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="1" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;">
<p class="lesson-label" style="margin:0 0 5px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#00180f;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 7 — RUN THIS — 6 MIN — NO PREP</p>
<p class="activity-title" style="margin:0 0 7px;font-size:15px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.35;color:#1a1a1a;">Tiny Brave Moves</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 9px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#333;">Students rank everyday acts of courage — low stakes, recognisable and easy to debate.</p>
<p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Read four: ask a teacher for help; join a group where you know nobody; admit you do not understand; apologise first after an argument.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Students show 1–4 fingers for which feels hardest. Pair-share: why did you choose that one?</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">3.</span>Ask: what makes a small brave action easier — preparation, another person, a first step, or just doing it quickly?</p>
<p class="takeaway" style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Courage does not require a dramatic situation. It can be practised in small moments where discomfort is real but the action still matters.</p>
<p class="fallback" style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Take a standing vote for just two options: ask for help vs apologise first.</p>
</div><div class="access-note" style="border-left:2px solid #7a9b96;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;">
<p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p>
<ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.45;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students can discuss the scenarios without revealing which one is personally difficult.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Voting by fingers avoids public movement if students prefer privacy.</li></ul></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="2" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 8 — RUN THIS — 7 MIN — MOVEMENT GAME</p><p class="activity-title">Rock–Paper–Scissors Evolution</p><p class="lesson-lead" style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:13px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.4;color:#1a1a1a;">Everyone starts as an egg. Find another egg, play rock-paper-scissors — the winner evolves to chicken, the loser stays an egg. Keep playing and evolving: egg → chicken → dinosaur → wizard.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Explain the chain and a physical action/sound for each stage (egg: crouched, hands together; chicken: flapping, clucking; dinosaur: roaring, claws; wizard: casting a spell). You can only play someone at your own stage — win to evolve up one stage, lose and drop back to egg.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>First wizards keep playing each other; last remaining eggs get a cheer, not a consequence. Two minutes flat out.</p><p style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#996600;">► </strong>Zest is showing up fully — the fastest way to lose isn’t losing a round, it’s playing it half-heartedly. The Courage here is small: keep re-entering the game after every loss, even as an egg again.</p><p style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Room too small to move? Play in pairs, seated, best of five rounds — same rock-paper-scissors, no evolving stages.</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students who don’t want to move around can play stationary rock-paper-scissors with one set partner for the full two minutes instead of seeking new opponents.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students with sensory sensitivities to noise or movement can be the round timer or scorekeeper.</li></ul></div></div><div class="panel" id="panel-14" style="display:none;"><div style="background:#00180f;padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:12px;border-radius:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;color:#7aaa7a;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Term 3  ·  Weeks 9–10</p><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:22px;font-weight:700;color:#f2b400;">Bravery &amp; Perseverance</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#a8c5a0;">Virtue: Courage · Strengths: Bravery · Perseverance</p></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="1" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#00180f;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 9 — RUN THIS — 6 MIN — 2 ERASERS</p><p class="activity-title" style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:15px;font-weight:700;color:#1a1a1a;">Eraser Race<span style="font-size:12px;font-weight:400;color:#666;margin-left:8px;">GGS Brain Breaks #15, p.34</span><span style="display:inline-block;background:#fff3cd;border:1px solid #c8960a;color:#7a5800;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;padding:1px 7px;border-radius:10px;margin-left:6px;">⚠ 2 whiteboard erasers</span></p><div style="float:right;margin:0 0 8px 14px;text-align:center;"><a href="Brain_breaks_ordered.pdf#page=31" style="display:block;margin-bottom:3px;" target="_blank" title="Open GGS Brain Breaks booklet p.34"><img alt="Open Brain Break activity in PDF, page 31" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," style="width:90px;border:1px solid #ccc;border-radius:3px;display:block;margin-bottom:3px;"/></a><span style="font-size:11px;color:#666;">p.34</span></div><p style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:15px;line-height:1.5;color:#1a1a1a;">Two students are nominated — one is the Racer, one is the Chaser. Both balance an eraser on their head. Racer gets a head start, then the walking race is on. If the eraser falls, stop and replace it.</p><div style="clear:both;"></div><div style="border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);margin-top:10px;padding-top:8px;"><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:12px;color:#555;"><strong style="color:#00180f;">Say it like this:</strong> 'Two volunteers. Balance an eraser on your head. One of you is the Racer, one is the Chaser. Racer gets a three-second head start. If the eraser falls — stop, replace it, keep going. Walking only.'</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;color:#666;">Why this lesson:</strong> Small physical risk, persistence when it falls — embodied Bravery &amp; Perseverance.</p></div><p style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> No erasers? Use any soft flat object such as a pencil case lid or folded sheet of paper — or run the seated version.</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students with balance difficulties compete in a seated version — eraser on the knee, shuffling or wheeling.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Any soft flat object works if erasers aren't available.</li></ul></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="2" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 10 — RUN THIS — 7 MIN — SHORT CLIP</p><p class="activity-title">What Is Courage?</p><p class="lesson-lead" style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:13px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.4;color:#1a1a1a;">Play: What Is Courage? — School of Life — <a href="https://youtu.be/H14bBuluwB8" style="color:#1565c0;text-decoration:underline;" target="_blank">youtu.be/H14bBuluwB8</a> (3:30). Courage is acting despite fear — not without it.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Play the clip (3:30). Groups of 3: each person names one thing they've kept going with even when it was hard — not finished, just kept going.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>That's the Perseverance strength. What made you stay?</p><p style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Bravery and Perseverance are not fixed qualities. Both can be practised: acting even when something feels uncomfortable, and choosing to keep going when progress is slow.</p><p style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Video won't load? Ask: 'What's the difference between being fearless and being brave?' Take 2–3 responses, then go to groups.</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">'Kept going with something hard' can feel exposing.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Offer: 'It can be something small — a game level, a habit, a friendship you worked at.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Doesn't have to be impressive.'</li></ul></div></div><div class="panel" id="panel-15" style="display:none;"><div style="background:#00180f;padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:12px;border-radius:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;color:#7aaa7a;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Term 4  ·  Weeks 1–2</p><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:22px;font-weight:700;color:#f2b400;">Honesty &amp; Courage review</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#a8c5a0;">Virtue: Courage · Strength: Honesty · Weekly focus: Courage review</p></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="1" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#00180f;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 1 — RUN THIS — 7 MIN — NO PREP</p><p class="activity-title">Say It or Stay Quiet?</p><p class="lesson-lead" style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:13px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.4;color:#1a1a1a;">Read out a scenario. Students vote by moving to one side of the room: “I’d say it” or “I’d stay quiet” — then a few explain why.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Scenarios (pick 2–3): “A friend asks if you like their new haircut and you don’t.” “You notice a group project teammate hasn’t done their part and the deadline’s tomorrow.” “Someone repeats a rumour about you that isn’t true, in front of you.” Students physically move to their side after each one.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Ask a couple of students from each side: what made you choose that? Is it about the person, the topic, or how confident you feel?</p><p style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#996600;">► </strong>Honesty isn’t automatic — it takes Courage, and the amount needed changes with the situation and the person. Naming that gap out loud is the first step to closing it.</p><p style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> No room to move? Use thumbs up/down instead of physically switching sides.</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students who don’t want to physically move can indicate their choice with a show of hands or a coloured card instead.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Keep scenarios light and impersonal — no one should be asked to reveal something about an actual current situation.</li></ul></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="2" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 2 — RUN THIS — 7 MIN — NO PREP</p><p class="activity-title">Honest, Kind, Both or Neither?</p><p class="lesson-lead" style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:13px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.4;color:#1a1a1a;">Groups of 3. Three statements — for each one decide: honest, kind, both, or neither? You must all agree before moving on.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Statements: 1. 'Your presentation was fine.' (when it wasn't) 2. 'I don't think that will work — here's why.' 3. 'I don't want to say anything that might hurt your feelings.' Discuss each.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Courage is what makes an honest conversation feel like investment rather than criticism, instead of just an excuse to be blunt.</p><p style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Honesty works best with care. Courage is what helps you say something true when staying quiet would be easier; judgement helps you choose how to say it so it can actually be heard.</p><p style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Groups won't agree? 'Where did you disagree — and what does that reveal about what you each value?'</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students who need processing time write their individual answer first — honest, kind, both, or neither — before the group discusses.</li></ul></div></div><div class="panel" id="panel-16" style="display:none;"><div style="background:#00180f;padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:12px;border-radius:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;color:#7aaa7a;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Term 4  ·  Weeks 3–4</p><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:22px;font-weight:700;color:#f2b400;">Temperance &amp; Prudence</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#a8c5a0;">Virtue: Temperance · Strength: Prudence</p></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="1" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#00180f;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 3 — RUN THIS — 6 MIN — NO PREP</p><p class="activity-title" style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:15px;font-weight:700;color:#1a1a1a;">Boi-ing 7<span style="font-size:12px;font-weight:400;color:#666;margin-left:8px;">GGS Brain Breaks #6, p.21</span></p><div style="float:right;margin:0 0 8px 14px;text-align:center;"><a href="Brain_breaks_ordered.pdf#page=18" style="display:block;margin-bottom:3px;" target="_blank" title="Open GGS Brain Breaks booklet p.21"><img alt="Open Brain Break activity in PDF, page 18" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," style="width:90px;border:1px solid #ccc;border-radius:3px;display:block;margin-bottom:3px;"/></a><span style="font-size:11px;color:#666;">p.21</span></div><p style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:15px;line-height:1.5;color:#1a1a1a;">Standing circle. Count up from 1. Every number containing 7 or divisible by 7, say 'boi-ing' instead. If someone gets it wrong, restart.</p><div style="clear:both;"></div><div style="border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);margin-top:10px;padding-top:8px;"><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:12px;color:#555;"><strong style="color:#00180f;">Say it like this:</strong> 'Standing circle. We count up from one. Every time you hit a number with a 7 in it — or a multiple of 7 — say boi-ing instead. So: one, two, three, four, five, six, boi-ing, eight. If two people speak at once or someone says the wrong thing, we restart at one.'</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;color:#666;">Why this lesson:</strong> Literally practises inhibitory control — the cognitive core of Self-Regulation.</p></div><p style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Too hard? Use multiples of 5 instead of 7 for the first round, then return to 7 if the group is ready.</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students with dyscalculia or number-processing difficulties can be the restart caller — when an error happens they call restart.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Active role, no number pressure.</li></ul></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="2" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 4 — RUN THIS — 7 MIN — SHORT CLIP</p><p class="activity-title">Marshmallow Strategy</p><p class="lesson-lead" style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:13px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.4;color:#1a1a1a;">Play: The Marshmallow Test animated — <a href="https://youtu.be/QX_oy9614HQ" style="color:#1565c0;text-decoration:underline;" target="_blank">youtu.be/QX_oy9614HQ</a> (3 min). The finding isn't about marshmallows — what's it actually about?</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Play the clip (3 min). Pairs: 'What strategy did the children who waited use? Name the actual technique — not that they were patient, what did they do?' 60 seconds.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>The useful idea is strategy: changing attention, environment or timing can make self-control easier. Prudence is planning those supports before the difficult moment arrives.</p><p style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Self-Regulation can be strengthened with strategies, not just effort. Prudence is thinking ahead far enough to choose the strategy before you need it.</p><p style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Video won't load? Describe the experiment briefly: kids, marshmallow, 15-minute wait for two. Ask: 'What strategies would actually work?' Groups brainstorm.</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Before playing, prime students: 'Watch for what the kids who waited actually do — not their faces, their actions.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">What strategy do they use?'</li></ul></div></div><div class="panel" id="panel-17" style="display:none;"><div style="background:#00180f;padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:12px;border-radius:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;color:#7aaa7a;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Term 4  ·  Weeks 5–6</p><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:22px;font-weight:700;color:#f2b400;">Humility &amp; Self-Regulation</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#a8c5a0;">Virtue: Temperance · Strengths: Humility · Self-Regulation</p></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="1" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#00180f;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 5 — RUN THIS — 6 MIN — NO PREP</p><p class="activity-title">Credit Where It’s Due</p><p class="lesson-lead">Start with something you can do well, then trace one piece of the credit back to somebody else.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Pairs. Person A names one skill they are reasonably good at — anything from gaming to cooking, sport, drawing, explaining things or staying calm.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Before A reveals the answer, Person B gets two guesses: who or what probably helped them get better at it? Then A names one real influence.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">3.</span>Swap. Finish with 3–4 volunteers naming only the influence, not the skill.</p><p class="takeaway" style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Humility is not pretending you have no strengths. It is seeing your abilities accurately and remembering that growth rarely happens entirely alone.</p><p class="fallback" style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Teacher models one skill and one person, opportunity or piece of feedback that helped build it.</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students can describe what the person did without naming them.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Reminder for students who aren't sure this applies to them: 'It can be anyone — a coach, a peer, someone who said one thing at the right moment.'</li></ul></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="2" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 6 — RUN THIS — 6 MIN — OPEN REFLECT</p><p class="activity-title">Equal Breathing</p><p class="lesson-lead" style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:13px;font-weight:400;color:#1a1a1a;">Microsoft Reflect: Equal Breathing<span style="font-size:12px;font-weight:400;color:#666;margin-left:8px;">reflect.microsoft.com/app/brainbreak/equalBreathing</span></p><p style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:15px;line-height:1.5;color:#1a1a1a;">Open on the classroom screen. A guided equal-breathing exercise that practises deliberately slowing and regulating attention.</p><div style="clear:both;"></div><div style="border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);margin-top:10px;padding-top:8px;"><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;color:#666;">Why this lesson:</strong> A short, concrete Self-Regulation practice students can use before a stressful or high-energy moment.</p><a href="https://reflect.microsoft.com/app/brainbreak/equalBreathing" style="display:inline-block;margin-top:10px;background:#1565c0;color:#fff;font-size:13px;font-weight:700;padding:5px 13px;border-radius:4px;text-decoration:none;" target="_blank">▶ Open Reflect Activity</a></div><p style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Reflect unavailable? Run a 60-second quiet reset: feet on the floor, eyes open or lowered, notice five things you can see and three things you can feel.</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students with respiratory conditions or anxiety triggered by breathing exercises should not be required to participate — they can sit quietly or write during the activity.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Never require breath-focused activities.</li></ul></div></div><div class="panel" id="panel-18" style="display:none;"><div style="background:#00180f;padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:12px;border-radius:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;color:#7aaa7a;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Term 4  ·  Weeks 7–8</p><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:22px;font-weight:700;color:#f2b400;">Forgiveness &amp; Year in review</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#a8c5a0;">Virtue: Temperance · Strength: Forgiveness · Weekly focus: Year in review</p></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="1" style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#00180f;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 7 — RUN THIS — 6 MIN — NO PREP</p><p class="activity-title">Six-Word Year</p><p class="lesson-lead">Summarise the entire school year in exactly six words. Funny, serious and mixed answers all count.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Give everyone 60 seconds to create exactly six words. Examples of form only: ‘Started nervous, ended knowing my people.’ or ‘Busy, weird, better than I expected.’</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Pairs check the word count. If it is not six, edit it until it is.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#00180f;margin-right:6px;">3.</span>Hear volunteers. The only response from the room is two claps — no questions and no explanation required.</p><p class="takeaway" style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Reflection does not have to become a long personal discussion. A tight constraint can make space for a complicated year without requiring anyone to unpack it publicly.</p><p class="fallback" style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Make it three words instead of six.</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students who don’t want to write or share can simply say ‘pass’ — the silent thinking time still counts as participation.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Offer a sentence stem on the board for students who find open-ended writing difficult to start.</li></ul></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="2" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 8 — RUN THIS — 7 MIN — SHORT CLIP</p><p class="activity-title">Friend Test</p><p class="lesson-lead" style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:13px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.4;color:#1a1a1a;">Play: Kristin Neff — Self-Compassion animated — <a href="https://youtu.be/11U0h0DPu7k" style="color:#1565c0;text-decoration:underline;" target="_blank">youtu.be/11U0h0DPu7k</a> (3:30). We treat ourselves worse than strangers — why?</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">1.</span> Play the clip (3:30). Then use a small fictional mistake: ‘A student forgot an important deadline and let their group down.’ Pairs give the response they would offer a friend.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">2.</span> Now ask: would people usually speak to themselves the same way after making that mistake? What changes when the person is you?</p><p style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#7a5800;">► </strong>Self-compassion is not pretending a mistake did not matter. It is responding firmly enough to learn from it without adding unnecessary self-attack.</p><p style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Video won't load? Ask: 'Why is it easier to forgive other people than to forgive yourself?'</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">The debrief is genuinely sensitive.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Reassure students: 'It can be something small, or something from earlier this year.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">You don't have to share anything serious.' Pause the clip at 1:00: 'She's saying we treat ourselves worse than a stranger.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Do you agree?'</li></ul></div></div><div class="panel" id="panel-19" style="display:none;"><div style="background:#00180f;padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:12px;border-radius:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;color:#7aaa7a;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Term 4  ·  Weeks 9–10</p><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:22px;font-weight:700;color:#f2b400;">Looking ahead &amp; Celebrate and reset</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#a8c5a0;">Weekly focuses: Looking ahead · Celebrate and reset</p></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="1" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 9 — RUN THIS — 6 MIN — NO PREP</p><p class="activity-title">One Step, Not the Whole Staircase</p><p class="lesson-lead" style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:13px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.4;color:#1a1a1a;">Week 9 — Looking ahead: One step, not the whole staircase</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Each student thinks of one thing they want to be different next year — one subject, one habit, one relationship. Not a list. One thing. No writing needed.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Pairs: 'What is the smallest first step you could take in the first week back?' 60 seconds each. The step has to be small enough that they would actually do it.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">3.</span>Rapid-fire around the room — first steps only, not the goals. No repeating.</p><p style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#996600;">► </strong>Looking ahead is not predicting the year — it is choosing the first move. That is what makes Hope a strength rather than a mood: people who can name a small first step are the ones who start.</p><p style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Short on time? Ask the room one question — 'What's one thing you'd like next year to look like?' — and take five answers.</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">No writing is required at any point — thinking it and saying it is enough.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students who would rather not share a personal goal can name one for the class or for Home Group instead.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">'Pass' is a normal answer in the rapid-fire round and does not need a reason.</li></ul></div><div class="lesson-card" data-week="2" style="background:#fffbee;border-left:4px solid #c8960a;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">WEEK 10 — RUN THIS — 7 MIN — NO PREP</p><p class="activity-title">Two Claps and a Thank You</p><p class="lesson-lead" style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:13px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.4;color:#1a1a1a;">Week 10 — Celebrate and reset: Two claps and a thank you</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">1.</span>Around the room: each student names one thing that went well this year — theirs or someone else's. The class gives two claps after each one. Keep it moving; no explanations.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">2.</span>Then everyone silently picks one person in the room to thank. Give 30 seconds to actually say it — go over and say it, or call it across the room.</p><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:#444;"><span style="font-weight:700;color:#7a5800;margin-right:6px;">3.</span>Close standing: one breath in, one breath out. 'That's the year.'</p><p style="margin:10px 0 0;font-size:13px;color:#5d4037;border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);padding-top:8px;"><strong style="color:#996600;">► </strong>Celebrating and resetting are the same move: you name what was good so you can put the rest down. Gratitude said out loud lands differently from gratitude that stays in your head.</p><p style="margin:6px 0 0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;">Fast fallback:</strong> Running out of time? Do step 1 only — one thing that went well, two claps, next person.</p></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students can pass at any step without giving a reason.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">A thank you can be written on a scrap of paper and handed over instead of said aloud.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Step 3 is optional — never require a breath-focused activity; standing quietly for a moment is enough.</li></ul></div><details class="more-options"><summary>Optional end-of-year brain break</summary><div style="background:#e8f5e9;border-left:4px solid #00180f;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0;padding:13px 16px;margin-bottom:4px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#00180f;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">OPTIONAL SWAP — 6 MIN — BRAIN BREAK</p><p style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:15px;font-weight:700;color:#1a1a1a;">Hand Clapping Games<span style="font-size:12px;font-weight:400;color:#666;margin-left:8px;">GGS Brain Breaks #22, p.45</span></p><div style="float:right;margin:0 0 8px 14px;text-align:center;"><a href="Brain_breaks_ordered.pdf#page=42" style="display:block;margin-bottom:3px;" target="_blank" title="Open GGS Brain Breaks booklet p.45"><img alt="Open Brain Break activity in PDF, page 42" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," style="width:90px;border:1px solid #ccc;border-radius:3px;display:block;margin-bottom:3px;"/></a><span style="font-size:11px;color:#666;">p.45</span></div><p style="margin:0 0 10px;font-size:15px;line-height:1.5;color:#1a1a1a;">Clap a short rhythm; the class claps it straight back. Build up to four or five patterns, each a little harder. Then pairs invent their own clapping routine, rehearse it and perform it to another pair.</p><div style="clear:both;"></div><div style="border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.08);margin-top:10px;padding-top:8px;"><p style="margin:0 0 4px;font-size:12px;color:#555;"><strong style="color:#00180f;">Say it like this:</strong> 'Watch and listen, then clap it straight back to me.' Do three or four, getting harder each time. 'Now in pairs — two minutes to invent your own routine and teach it to each other. We'll watch a few before the bell.'</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:12px;color:#666;font-style:italic;"><strong style="font-style:normal;color:#666;">Why this lesson:</strong> Everyone succeeds on the first try and the room ends on a lift — the right note for the last fortnight of the year.</p></div></div><div style="border-left:2px solid #b2dfdb;padding:4px 10px 4px 12px;margin-bottom:10px;"><p style="margin:0 0 3px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;color:#557b76;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;font-family:Trebuchet MS,sans-serif;">Accessibility</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:14px;font-size:11px;color:#666;line-height:1.4;"><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Students who cannot clap can tap a desk, click fingers or stamp — any sound on the beat counts.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Noise-sensitive students: run the whole thing as quiet finger clicks, or let them keep the rhythm visually without sound.</li><li style="margin-bottom:2px;">Hearing impairment: stand where they can watch your hands; the pattern is fully visible.</li></ul></div></details></div></div>
</div>
<script>
const TABS=[{"i":0,"term":1,"weeks":"1–2","title":"Welcome back &amp; Positive Education intro","start":"2026-01-26","end":"2026-02-06"},{"i":1,"term":1,"weeks":"3–4","title":"PERMAH overview &amp; Virtues &amp; character strengths","start":"2026-02-09","end":"2026-02-20"},{"i":2,"term":1,"weeks":"5–6","title":"Humanity &amp; Love","start":"2026-02-23","end":"2026-03-06"},{"i":3,"term":1,"weeks":"7–8","title":"Social Intelligence &amp; Kindness","start":"2026-03-09","end":"2026-03-20"},{"i":4,"term":1,"weeks":"9–10","title":"Transcendence &amp; Humour","start":"2026-03-23","end":"2026-04-03"},{"i":5,"term":2,"weeks":"1–2","title":"Spirituality / Meaning &amp; Appreciation of Beauty","start":"2026-04-20","end":"2026-05-01"},{"i":6,"term":2,"weeks":"3–4","title":"Hope &amp; Gratitude","start":"2026-05-04","end":"2026-05-15"},{"i":7,"term":2,"weeks":"5–6","title":"Wisdom &amp; Love of Learning","start":"2026-05-18","end":"2026-05-29"},{"i":8,"term":2,"weeks":"7–8","title":"Perspective &amp; Curiosity","start":"2026-06-01","end":"2026-06-12"},{"i":9,"term":2,"weeks":"9–10","title":"Creativity &amp; Judgement","start":"2026-06-15","end":"2026-06-26"},{"i":10,"term":3,"weeks":"1–2","title":"Virtues recap &amp; PERMAH recap","start":"2026-07-13","end":"2026-07-24"},{"i":11,"term":3,"weeks":"3–4","title":"Justice &amp; Leadership","start":"2026-07-27","end":"2026-08-07"},{"i":12,"term":3,"weeks":"5–6","title":"Fairness &amp; Teamwork","start":"2026-08-10","end":"2026-08-21"},{"i":13,"term":3,"weeks":"7–8","title":"Courage &amp; Zest","start":"2026-08-24","end":"2026-09-04"},{"i":14,"term":3,"weeks":"9–10","title":"Bravery &amp; Perseverance","start":"2026-09-07","end":"2026-09-18"},{"i":15,"term":4,"weeks":"1–2","title":"Honesty &amp; Courage review","start":"2026-10-05","end":"2026-10-16"},{"i":16,"term":4,"weeks":"3–4","title":"Temperance &amp; Prudence","start":"2026-10-19","end":"2026-10-30"},{"i":17,"term":4,"weeks":"5–6","title":"Humility &amp; Self-Regulation","start":"2026-11-02","end":"2026-11-13"},{"i":18,"term":4,"weeks":"7–8","title":"Forgiveness &amp; Year in review","start":"2026-11-16","end":"2026-11-27"},{"i":19,"term":4,"weeks":"9–10","title":"Looking ahead &amp; Celebrate and reset","start":"2026-11-30","end":"2026-12-11"}];
let cur=0;
function pd(s){const[y,m,d]=s.split('-').map(Number);return new Date(y,m-1,d);}
function status(t){
  const now=new Date();now.setHours(0,0,0,0);
  const e=pd(t.end);e.setHours(23,59,59);
  return now>=pd(t.start)&&now<=e?'current':now>e?'past':'future';
}

function weekInPanel(t){
  const now=new Date();now.setHours(0,0,0,0);
  const start=pd(t.start);start.setHours(0,0,0,0);
  const diff=Math.floor((now-start)/86400000);
  return diff<=6?1:2;
}
function highlightWeek(){
  document.querySelectorAll('.lesson-card').forEach(c=>c.classList.remove('this-week'));
  const t=TABS[cur];
  if(status(t)!=='current') return;
  const w=weekInPanel(t);
  const panel=document.getElementById('panel-'+cur);
  const primary=panel.querySelector('.lesson-card[data-week="'+w+'"]:not([data-optional="true"])');
  if(primary) primary.classList.add('this-week');
}
function buildNav(){
  const termRow=document.getElementById('term-row');
  const tabRow=document.getElementById('tab-row');
  const TN={1:'Term 1',2:'Term 2',3:'Term 3',4:'Term 4'};
  let lastTerm=0;const termEls={};
  TABS.forEach((t,i)=>{
    if(t.term!==lastTerm){
      const c=document.createElement('div');
      c.className='term-hdr';c.textContent=TN[t.term];
      termRow.appendChild(c);termEls[t.term]=c;lastTerm=t.term;
    }
    const btn=document.createElement('button');
    btn.className='tab'+(status(t)==='past'?' past':'');
    btn.id='tab-'+i;btn.textContent='T'+t.term+' · W'+t.weeks;btn.setAttribute('role','tab');btn.setAttribute('aria-controls','panel-'+i);
    btn.onclick=()=>show(i);tabRow.appendChild(btn);
  });
  requestAnimationFrame(()=>{
    const ws={};
    TABS.forEach((t,i)=>{const el=document.getElementById('tab-'+i);if(el)ws[t.term]=(ws[t.term]||0)+el.offsetWidth+3;});
    Object.entries(ws).forEach(([t,w])=>{if(termEls[t])termEls[t].style.minWidth=w+'px';});
  });
}
function show(idx){
  document.querySelectorAll('.panel').forEach(p=>p.style.display='none');
  document.querySelectorAll('.tab').forEach(t=>t.classList.remove('active'));
  document.getElementById('panel-'+idx).style.display='block';
  const tab=document.getElementById('tab-'+idx);
  tab.classList.add('active');document.querySelectorAll('.tab').forEach(x=>x.setAttribute('aria-selected','false'));tab.setAttribute('aria-selected','true');
  tab.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth',block:'nearest',inline:'center'});
  cur=idx;updateBanner();highlightWeek();
  document.getElementById('prev-btn').disabled=idx===0;
  document.getElementById('next-btn').disabled=idx===TABS.length-1;
}
function updateBanner(){
  const t=TABS[cur],s=status(t);
  const badge=s==='current'
    ?'<span style="background:#f2b400;color:#00180f;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:10px;margin-right:8px;">▶ THIS WEEK</span>'
    :s==='past'
    ?'<span style="background:#e0e0e0;color:#777;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:10px;margin-right:8px;">COMPLETED</span>'
    :'<span style="background:#e8f5e9;color:#00180f;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;padding:2px 8px;border-radius:10px;margin-right:8px;">UPCOMING</span>';
  const exact=(s==='current')?' · Week '+(parseInt(t.weeks.split('–')[0])+weekInPanel(t)-1):''; document.getElementById('banner-text').innerHTML=badge+'<strong>'+t.title+'</strong>&nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;Term '+t.term+', Weeks '+t.weeks+exact;
}
function navigate(d){const n=cur+d;if(n>=0&&n<TABS.length)show(n);}
buildNav();
let target=TABS.length-1;
for(let i=0;i<TABS.length;i++){
  if(status(TABS[i])==='current'){target=i;break;}
  if(status(TABS[i])==='future'){target=i;break;}
}
show(target);
</script>
</body>
</html>